Docker Volume Persistence
1)Create the Docker Volume:                    docker volume create data_vol
  to check volumes:                              docker volume ls
2)Run a container and mount the volume:        docker run --name container1 -v data_vol:/data -it alpine
3)Create a file inside the volume and exit:    echo "Hello from the volume!" > /data/testfile.txt
4)Remove the first Container:                  docker rm container1
5)Start a new container using the same volume: docker run --name container2 -v data_vol:/data -it alpine
6)Verify the file still exists:                cat /data/testfile.txt


Dockerfile – Python Application
1)Create the Python Script:  app.py
2)Create the Dockerfile:   
# Use the official lightweight Python image
FROM python:3.9-slim
# Set the working directory inside the container
WORKDIR /app
# Copy the local script into the container
COPY app.py .
# Command to run the script
CMD ["python", "app.py"]
                  
3)Build the Image:                             docker build -t my-python-app .
4)Run the Container:                           docker run my-python-app



Dockerfile -java
1)java file: main.java
// Main.java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello! Java is running inside Docker.");
    }
}

2)Create Dockerfile: 
# Use an official OpenJDK runtime as a parent image
FROM openjdk:17-jdk-slim
# Set the working directory inside the container
WORKDIR /app
# Copy the Java source file into the container
COPY Main.java .
# Compile the Java program
RUN javac Main.java
# Run the compiled class
CMD ["java", "Main"]

3)docker build -t my-java-app .
4)docker run my-java-app


Docker volume with MYSQL
1)Run the MySQL Container with a Volume:  docker run --name my-mysql -e MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=my-secret-pw -v mysql_data:/var/lib/mysql -d mysql:latest
2)Access MySQL and Insert Data:           docker exec -it my-mysql mysql -u root -p 
SQL:
CREATE DATABASE testdb;
USE testdb;
CREATE TABLE users (id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(50));
INSERT INTO users (name) VALUES ('Alice'), ('Bob');
SELECT * FROM users;
EXIT;
3)Stop and Remove the Container:                   docker stop my-mysql
                                                   docker rm my-mysql
4)Start a New Container Using the Same Volume:     docker run --name my-mysql-new -e MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=my-secret-pw -v mysql_data:/var/lib/mysql -d mysql:latest
5)Verify Data Persistence:
use testdb;
select * from users;


Dockerfile: nginx web server
1) Create your HTML file: index.html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>My Docker Web Server</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Hello from Nginx!</h1>
    <p>This page is being served from inside a Docker container.</p>
</body>
</html>
2) Create Dockerfile:     
# Use the official Nginx image
FROM nginx:latest
# Copy your custom HTML file to the Nginx default directory
COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html
# Expose port 80
EXPOSE 80
3)Build the Image :       docker build -t my-custom-nginx .
4)Run the Container:      docker run --name web-server1 -d -p 8082:80 my-custom-nginx
5) verify:
In your browser: http://localhost:8082.
In the terminal: curl localhost:8082


